Property/function Purpose <code>transform</code> Applies one or more 2D/3D transform functions <code>translate()</code> Moves an element along axes <code>rotate()</code> Rotates around an axis <code>scale()</code> Changes drawn size <code>skew()</code> Slants drawn geometry <code>transform-origin</code> Sets the point used for a transform <code>perspective</code> Adds depth perspective for 3D transforms <pre><code>.card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-0.2rem);
}
</code></pre> <p>Transforms do not make surrounding content reflow to the transformed position. They can create stacking contexts and affect overflow.</p>

Element Meaning Important attributes <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> A collection where order is not meaningful Global attributes <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> A sequence where order matters <code>start</code>, <code>reversed</code>, <code>type</code> <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> An item in a list <code>value</code> can set its number in an ordered list <p><code>&lt;ol type&gt;</code> can use decimal numbers (<code>1</code>), letters (<code>a</code> or <code>A</code>), or Roman numerals (<code>i</code> or <code>I</code>). CSS is usually a better choice for visual marker styling.</p> <pre><code>&lt;ol&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Write the page structure.&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Add meaningful content.&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Style the page with CSS.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
</code></pre> <p>Lists can be nested when the parent-child relationship is meaningful. Put the nested <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> inside the relevant <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>.</p>

Element Meaning and example <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>–<code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> Headings by document hierarchy; use levels to express structure <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> Paragraph <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> Strong importance, seriousness, or urgency <code>&lt;em&gt;</code> Stress emphasis that can affect sentence meaning <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code> Text relevant to the current context <code>&lt;small&gt;</code> Side comments or fine print <code>&lt;abbr title="..."&gt;</code> Abbreviation with an optional expansion <code>&lt;time datetime="..."&gt;</code> Date/time with machine-readable value <code>&lt;code&gt;</code> Short code fragment <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> Preformatted text, often used with <code>code</code> <code>&lt;blockquote cite="..."&gt;</code> Extended quotation <code>&lt;q cite="..."&gt;</code> Short inline quotation <code>&lt;cite&gt;</code> Title of a creative work <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;figcaption&gt;</code> Self-contained media/content and its caption <code>&lt;dl&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;dt&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;dd&gt;</code> Description list, term, and description <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>&lt;article&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;How DNS lookup works&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DNS&lt;/strong&gt; maps names to records used to locate services.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;figure&gt;
    &lt;img src="/images/dns-flow.svg" alt="A resolver asking DNS servers for a record"&gt;
    &lt;figcaption&gt;A simplified DNS lookup.&lt;/figcaption&gt;
  &lt;/figure&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Read the &lt;a href="/networking/dns"&gt;DNS lesson&lt;/a&gt; for details.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/article&gt;
</code></pre>

Element Purpose <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> Contains tabular data <code>&lt;caption&gt;</code> Gives the table a concise title or purpose <code>&lt;thead&gt;</code> Groups header rows <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code> Groups the main data rows <code>&lt;tfoot&gt;</code> Groups summary or footer rows <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code> Creates a row <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> Header cell; <code>scope</code> can identify a row or column header <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> Data cell <pre><code>&lt;table&gt;
  &lt;caption&gt;Weekly study plan&lt;/caption&gt;
  &lt;thead&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;th scope="col"&gt;Day&lt;/th&gt;
      &lt;th scope="col"&gt;Topic&lt;/th&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/thead&gt;
  &lt;tbody&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;th scope="row"&gt;Monday&lt;/th&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;HTML forms&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
</code></pre> <p>Use <code>scope="col"</code> or <code>scope="row"</code> for straightforward header relationships. Complex tables may need <code>id</code> and <code>headers</code> associations or, preferably, a simpler structure.</p>

Method Use Result / mutation <code>forEach(callback)</code> Perform an action for each item Returns <code>undefined</code>; does not build a result array <code>map(callback)</code> Transform each visited item New array <code>filter(callback)</code> Keep items whose callback result is truthy New array <code>find(callback)</code> Find the first matching item Item or <code>undefined</code> <code>some(callback)</code> / <code>every(callback)</code> Test one / all items Boolean <code>reduce(callback, initialValue)</code> Accumulate a result One accumulated value <code>includes(value)</code> Check for a value Boolean <code>slice(start, end)</code> Copy a range New array; original unchanged <code>splice(start, count, ...items)</code> Remove or insert items Mutates original; returns removed items <p>Callbacks commonly receive <code>(element, index, array)</code>. Use <code>map</code> for a transformation, <code>filter</code> for selection, and <code>forEach</code> for side effects. Always supply an initial value to <code>reduce</code> when an empty array is possible.</p> <pre><code>const orders = [{ total: 12 }, { total: 25 }, { total: 8 }];
const expensiveTotals = orders
  .filter((order) =&gt; order.total &gt;= 10)
  .map((order) =&gt; order.total);

const total = orders.reduce((sum, order) =&gt; sum + order.total, 0);
</code></pre> <p>This is method chaining: each returned value becomes the receiver for the next method.</p>

Method Use <code>console.debug(...values)</code> Detailed debugging output; may be filtered by default <code>console.log(...values)</code> General diagnostic output <code>console.info(...values)</code> Informational message <code>console.warn(...values)</code> Warning that deserves attention <code>console.error(...values)</code> Error diagnostic; does not itself throw an exception <pre><code>console.debug("Selected item:", item);
console.info("Request completed");
console.warn("Using a fallback value");
console.error("Could not load profile", error);
</code></pre>

Method Use <code>console.table(data, columns?)</code> Display suitable arrays or objects as a table <code>console.assert(condition, ...values)</code> Log a message if the condition is falsy; it does not stop execution <code>console.trace(...values)</code> Log a stack trace from the call site <pre><code>console.table(
  [{ name: "Notebook", price: 4 }, { name: "Pen", price: 2 }],
  ["name", "price"],
);
console.assert(items.length &gt; 0, "Expected at least one item");
</code></pre> <p>The exact object-inspection interface depends on the browser DevTools. Do not assume logged objects are immutable snapshots; consoles may show their current state when expanded.</p>

Method Use <code>console.group(...label)</code> Start an expanded nested group <code>console.groupCollapsed(...label)</code> Start a collapsed nested group <code>console.groupEnd()</code> Close the current group <code>console.count(label?)</code> Log and increment a counter for a label <code>console.countReset(label?)</code> Reset a counter <pre><code>console.group("Form submission");
console.count("attempt");
console.log("Validating fields");
console.groupEnd();
</code></pre> <p>Groups can be nested. Keep labels consistent when incrementing and resetting counters; omitted labels use a default counter.</p>

Method Use <code>console.time(label?)</code> Start a timer <code>console.timeLog(label?, ...values)</code> Log elapsed time without ending the timer <code>console.timeEnd(label?)</code> Log elapsed time and end the timer <code>console.timeStamp(label?)</code> Add a timestamp marker where supported by DevTools <pre><code>console.time("load");
await loadData();
console.timeLog("load", "data received");
renderData();
console.timeEnd("load");
</code></pre> <p>Timers use labels and are intended for lightweight diagnostics, not repeatable benchmarking. Use the Performance panel or a benchmark harness for deeper measurement. <code>timeStamp()</code> support and its appearance depend on the browser's developer tools.</p>

API Behavior <code>setTimeout(callback, delay)</code> Schedules a callback no earlier than a delay <code>clearTimeout(id)</code> Cancels a pending timeout <code>setInterval(callback, delay)</code> Repeatedly schedules callbacks <code>clearInterval(id)</code> Cancels a repeating timer <pre><code>const timerId = setTimeout(() =&gt; {
  console.log("The timer became eligible");
}, 1000);

clearTimeout(timerId);
</code></pre> <p>Timers are scheduling requests, not precise clocks: the callback runs only when the event loop can process it, and delays may be clamped by the browser. Avoid intervals that can overlap long-running async work; schedule the next timeout after each operation when sequential execution is needed.</p>

Method Purpose Returns <code>map(fn)</code> Transform every element New array <code>filter(fn)</code> Keep elements matching a predicate New array <code>find(fn)</code> Find first matching element Element or <code>undefined</code> <code>some(fn)</code> Test whether any element matches Boolean <code>every(fn)</code> Test whether all elements match Boolean <code>reduce(fn, initial)</code> Accumulate a result Accumulated value <code>forEach(fn)</code> Perform an action for each element <code>undefined</code> <code>includes(value)</code> Check for a matching value Boolean <code>slice(start, end)</code> Copy a range New array <code>splice(start, count, ...)</code> Remove/insert in the original array Removed items <pre><code>const orders = [
  { id: "a1", total: 12 },
  { id: "b2", total: 25 },
];

const expensiveIds = orders
  .filter((order) =&gt; order.total &gt;= 20)
  .map((order) =&gt; order.id);
</code></pre> <p><code>sort()</code> mutates the original array; copy first when immutability matters. Array methods such as <code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> return new arrays but do not deep copy contained objects. For callback signatures, practical method chaining, numeric sorting, and a detailed <code>reduce</code> example, see arrays and array methods.</p>

API Result <code>document.getElementById(id)</code> One matching element or <code>null</code> <code>document.querySelector(selector)</code> First CSS-selector match or <code>null</code> <code>document.querySelectorAll(selector)</code> Static <code>NodeList</code> of matches <code>element.closest(selector)</code> Closest matching ancestor, including itself, or <code>null</code> <pre><code>const title = document.querySelector("main h1");
const buttons = document.querySelectorAll("button");
</code></pre> <p><code>querySelectorAll()</code> returns a static NodeList: later DOM additions do not appear in that already-created list. A NodeList may be iterated with <code>for...of</code>; use <code>Array.from(nodes)</code> when array methods are needed.</p>

API Purpose <code>length</code> Number of UTF-16 code units; not always the number of visible characters <code>includes(value)</code> Test whether a substring occurs <code>startsWith(value)</code>, <code>endsWith(value)</code> Check a prefix or suffix <code>slice(start, end)</code> Return part of a string <code>trim()</code> Remove whitespace at both ends <code>toLowerCase()</code>, <code>toUpperCase()</code> Change letter casing <code>replace(search, replacement)</code> Replace a match; use <code>replaceAll</code> or a global regular expression for all matches <code>split(separator)</code> Produce an array of parts <pre><code>const email = "  MINA@example.com ";
const normalized = email.trim().toLowerCase();
</code></pre> <p>Do not treat <code>length</code> as a count of user-perceived characters for all Unicode text. Emoji and combined characters may occupy multiple code units.</p>
